COMMENT
  Guess my number game

  An ALGOL 68 version of a silly game I made on my programmable
  calculator when I was bored in math class in 1987, with a couple of
  additions like input validation and computer guesses.

  Copyright (c) 2026 Matthew Helmke for the ALGOL 68 version (this one)
     with assistance from Claude Code (claude.ai/code)
     using the claude-opus-5-5 model

  To run (ALGOL 68 Genie):
    a68g guessnumber.a68

  Language notes (ALGOL 68 choices a reader should not mistake for bugs):

    * Keywords are written in capitals, such as IF, FI, DO, and OD. This is
      upper stropping, the way a68g tells keywords from names. IF and CASE
      blocks close with the keyword reversed, which is where the Bourne
      shell got fi and esac.

    * AND evaluates both operands; it does not short-circuit as in C. The
      trim procedure therefore guards each character test with an IF, so an
      empty line is never indexed. a68g offers ANDTH as a short-circuit
      form, but it prints a warning on every run.

    * ALGOL 68 has no continue or break. Each turn is wrapped in IF blocks
      instead, and the loop ends when the gameover flag is set.

    * End of input is an event, not a return value. The procedure handed to
      on logical file end sets a flag, which the loop checks right after it
      reads a guess. The flag is cleared before each read, because reading
      the newline after a last line that has no newline also fires the event.

    * a68g seeds its random generator from the clock, to the second, when
      it starts, as the C version does with time(0). Two games started in
      the same second therefore pick the same number. Every run also writes
      the generator's state to a .Random.seed file in the current directory.

    * A form feed is the end of a page to a68g, not a character, so reading
      a line stops at one and the guess never contains it. The procedure
      handed to on page end sets a flag instead, and a line that set it is
      not a whole number.

    * The language has no trim and no string-to-integer conversion, so this
      file defines small procedures for both.

    * print pads an integer to a fixed width with a sign. whole (n, 0)
      prints it with no padding, the way the other versions do.

    * getenv is an ALGOL 68 Genie extension, not part of the 1968 language,
      and the only one this file uses; a68g --pedantic flags nothing else.
      It reads the GMN_SECRET parity hook, and returns an empty string when
      the variable is unset.

  This program is free software; you can redistribute it and/or
